"""In-depth material for the Operating Systems lessons."""
from learn import deepen

deepen("os-processes-threads",
       proofs=[
           ("fork() doubles the process count: n forks give 2ⁿ processes",
            """Let P(k) be the number of processes after k unconditional fork() calls executed by every process. Each existing process executes the next fork and produces exactly one child, so P(k + 1) = 2P(k) with P(0) = 1, giving P(n) = 2ⁿ; the number of **new** processes is 2ⁿ − 1. With a condition such as `if (fork() == 0) fork();` only the child (return value 0) executes the second fork: 1 process becomes 2, and the child makes one more: 3 in total. For a loop `for (i = 0; i < n; i++) fork();` the count is again 2ⁿ because every process, including children, continues the loop with the remaining iterations."""),
           ("Lost updates: the minimum final value of an unsynchronised shared counter is 2",
            """Two threads each execute n ≥ 2 times `count = count + 1`, compiled as load, add, store. Here is a schedule that ends with 2: thread A loads 0 and is paused; thread B runs n − 1 complete iterations (count = n − 1); A resumes and stores 1 (count = 1); B starts its last iteration and loads 1, then is paused; A runs its remaining n − 1 iterations (count climbs to n); B resumes and stores 1 + 1 = 2. Every increment of the other thread has been overwritten, so only two survive. Exhaustive enumeration of all interleavings for n = 2, 3, 4 confirms that 2 is the minimum possible final value and 2n the maximum (no interference)."""),
           ("Context-switch overhead and the useful fraction of the CPU",
            """If a time slice of length q is followed by a context switch costing s, each cycle has q useful time out of q + s, so the useful fraction is q/(q + s). With q = 10 ms and s = 1 ms the CPU does useful work 10/11 ≈ 90.9% of the time. As q shrinks, the fraction tends to zero (all time spent switching); as q grows, switching overhead vanishes but response time worsens. This explains why round-robin quanta of 10–100 ms are chosen (large compared with s, small compared with human perception)."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Process life cycle and kernel structures",
            """The five-state model: new, ready, running, waiting (blocked), terminated; suspend states add swapped-out versions of ready and waiting. The PCB stores PID, state, program counter, CPU registers, scheduling information, memory-management information, open files and accounting data. The **dispatcher** performs the context switch and returns to user mode at the right place; dispatch latency is its overhead. The long-term scheduler controls the degree of multiprogramming, the medium-term scheduler swaps processes out, and the short-term scheduler picks the next ready process."""),
           ("Threads, system calls and mode switches",
            """Threads share code, data, heap, open files and signal handlers but own registers, program counter and stack, so thread creation and switching are cheaper than for processes. User-level threads are invisible to the kernel: a blocking system call blocks the whole process and they cannot exploit multiple CPUs, but they switch without a mode change. Kernel threads provide true parallelism. A system call is a controlled entry into the kernel through a trap instruction; the CPU switches to kernel mode, runs the handler and returns; interrupts are asynchronous, traps and exceptions synchronous. `exec` replaces the process image, `wait` reaps a child (avoiding zombies) and an orphan is adopted by init."""),
       ])

deepen("os-cpu-scheduling",
       proofs=[
           ("SJF minimises the average waiting time among non-preemptive schedules (exchange argument)",
            """Suppose all processes arrive at time 0 with bursts b₁ … bₙ and run in some order. The process in position k waits for the sum of the bursts before it, so the total waiting time is Σ_{k=1}^{n} (n − k)·b_{(k)}: the burst in position k delays n − k later processes. To minimise the sum, the largest multipliers must be paired with the smallest bursts (rearrangement inequality), i.e. run the bursts in non-decreasing order. Formally, if a longer job immediately precedes a shorter one, swapping them changes the total waiting time by (b_short − b_long) < 0, so the schedule improves. Hence SJF is optimal; SRTF extends the argument to preemption and is optimal over all schedules, but needs knowledge of future burst lengths (estimated by exponential averaging τₙ₊₁ = α tₙ + (1 − α) τₙ)."""),
           ("Waiting-time identities: WT = TAT − BT and Little's formula intuition",
            """For process i with arrival Aᵢ, burst Bᵢ and completion Cᵢ: turnaround TATᵢ = Cᵢ − Aᵢ is the whole time in the system; it consists of the burst (time actually executing) plus the time spent ready but not running, so WTᵢ = TATᵢ − Bᵢ. Response time is the time to first execution minus arrival, so for non-preemptive FCFS, SJF response = waiting. Summing over all processes and averaging gives the averages used in the questions. Throughput is n divided by the makespan, and CPU utilisation is the busy time divided by the total time (idle gaps appear when nothing has arrived)."""),
           ("Round robin: bounded waiting",
            """With n processes and quantum q, a ready process waits at most (n − 1)·q before it next runs: at most n − 1 other processes can be ahead of it in the queue, and each runs for at most q before being preempted. Therefore the response time of a newly arrived process is at most (n − 1)q and no process starves. If q ≥ the longest burst, no process is preempted and RR degenerates to FCFS; as q → 0 RR approaches processor sharing at the price of context switches."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Priority scheduling, aging and multilevel queues",
            """Priority scheduling (preemptive or not) runs the highest-priority process; low-priority processes can **starve**; **aging** gradually raises the priority of waiting processes. A multilevel queue partitions processes into fixed classes (foreground round robin, background FCFS); a multilevel feedback queue lets processes move between queues based on their CPU use, favouring short and I/O-bound jobs. Real-time scheduling: rate-monotonic (static priorities, schedulable if utilisation ≤ n(2^(1/n) − 1)) and earliest-deadline-first (dynamic, schedulable if utilisation ≤ 1)."""),
           ("Tie-breaking and simulation conventions",
            """Unless told otherwise break ties by arrival time, then by process number. In RR, when a quantum expires at the same time as a new arrival, the new process joins the queue before the preempted one. Do not count idle time in waiting times; if the CPU is idle the clock jumps to the next arrival. Draw a Gantt chart with times, then compute CT, TAT = CT − AT, WT = TAT − BT. A preemptive algorithm needs the remaining time at each decision point; recompute it at every arrival."""),
       ])

deepen("os-synchronization",
       proofs=[
           ("Peterson's algorithm satisfies mutual exclusion",
            """Shared variables: flag[0], flag[1] (initially false) and turn. Process i sets flag[i] = true; turn = j; and then waits while (flag[j] && turn == j). Suppose both processes were in the critical section at the same time. Both flags are then true. Each process wrote turn once in its entry code (P₀ writes 1, P₁ writes 0) and neither writes it again until it leaves. Let the *later* of these two writes be P₁'s (the other case is symmetric); after it, turn = 0 for the rest of the time. P₁ passed its test only if flag[0] was false or turn ≠ 0; but flag[0] is true (P₀ set it before writing turn and is still inside), and turn = 0 after P₁'s own write — so P₁ must still be waiting, a contradiction. Hence mutual exclusion holds. Progress and bounded waiting follow because after leaving a process lowers its flag, letting the waiting one proceed, and a process that re-enters writes turn giving the other the priority, so it waits for at most one critical section."""),
           ("Semaphore arithmetic: the value counts available resources minus blocked processes",
            """Define the semaphore S with initial value S₀. wait(S) decrements S and blocks if the result is negative; signal(S) increments S and wakes one process if the result is ≤ 0. Invariant: S = S₀ − (#waits completed) + (#signals completed). When S ≥ 0 it equals the number of free units; when S < 0 its magnitude equals the number of blocked processes. Hence after 12 waits and 4 signals on a semaphore initialised to 5: 5 − 12 + 4 = −3, i.e. three processes are blocked. A binary semaphore used as a mutex alternates between 1 and 0 and should be initialised to 1."""),
           ("Deadlock in the bounded-buffer solution when the P operations are swapped",
            """The correct producer does P(empty) then P(mutex). If the order is reversed (P(mutex) then P(empty)) and the buffer is full: the producer takes the mutex and then blocks on empty, which can only be signalled by a consumer; the consumer needs the mutex to consume, which is held by the blocked producer, so neither can ever proceed: deadlock. The rule is to acquire the counting semaphore before the mutex, so that a process never blocks while holding the mutex."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Critical-section requirements and hardware support",
            """Mutual exclusion, progress (decisions about entering cannot be postponed indefinitely and only processes in their entry section participate) and bounded waiting. Software: strict alternation violates progress; Peterson works for two processes; the bakery algorithm for n. Hardware: TestAndSet and Swap (atomic read-modify-write) give mutual exclusion and progress but, in the simple spinlock, not bounded waiting; compare-and-swap builds lock-free structures. Disabling interrupts works only on uniprocessors and only for kernel code. Spinlocks waste CPU but avoid context switches on short critical sections."""),
           ("Classic problems",
            """Bounded buffer (mutex, empty = n, full = 0); readers–writers (first problem favours readers and can starve writers, second favours writers); dining philosophers (deadlock if everyone takes the left fork first; fixes: at most n − 1 at the table, pick up both forks atomically, or asymmetric ordering); sleeping barber; cigarette smokers. Monitors provide mutual exclusion automatically with condition variables (wait releases the monitor, signal wakes a waiter — Hoare vs Mesa semantics). Priority inversion occurs when a low-priority holder blocks a high-priority process while a medium-priority process runs; priority inheritance fixes it."""),
       ])

deepen("os-deadlocks",
       proofs=[
           ("Minimum resources that guarantee deadlock freedom: R ≥ n(k − 1) + 1",
            """Let n processes each need at most k units of one resource type, with R units in total. The worst case for deadlock is that every process holds k − 1 units and requests its last unit: that uses n(k − 1) units, and if R = n(k − 1) there are none free, so all are blocked and no one can finish. If R ≥ n(k − 1) + 1 then in any state where every process holds at most k − 1 units there is at least one free unit, and as soon as some process holds k units it can finish and release. More carefully: by the pigeonhole principle, if R ≥ n(k − 1) + 1, some process can always obtain its maximum need, complete and release, which then lets the others continue. For n = 4 and k = 3, R = 4·2 + 1 = 9 units are sufficient."""),
           ("The safe-state theorem: safe ⇒ no deadlock; deadlock ⇒ unsafe",
            """A state is safe if there is a sequence ⟨P₁, …, Pₙ⟩ in which each Pᵢ's remaining need can be met by the available resources plus those held by the earlier processes. If the state is safe, running the processes in that order lets each finish, so no process waits forever: no deadlock. A deadlocked state has a set of processes each waiting for resources held by others in the set; none can finish, so no safe sequence exists: it is unsafe. The converse of the first statement fails: an unsafe state may still avoid deadlock if processes happen to release resources early or never request their maximum. Banker's algorithm keeps the system safe by granting a request only if the resulting state is safe."""),
           ("Banker's safety algorithm is correct and runs in O(m n²)",
            """Work := Available. Repeat: find an unfinished process i with Needᵢ ≤ Work; set Work := Work + Allocationᵢ and mark i finished. If all processes finish, the order found is a safe sequence. Correctness: whenever the algorithm picks i it can really run to completion with the resources available then (Needᵢ ≤ Work), after which its allocation returns to the pool, so later choices are valid. If the algorithm gets stuck with unfinished processes, any remaining process needs more than Work; since processes that finished only add to Work, and finishing orders only grow Work monotonically, no other order can succeed either (the set of finishable processes is determined by a monotone closure). Each round scans n processes with m comparisons, and there are at most n rounds: O(m n²)."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("The four conditions and what breaks each",
            """Mutual exclusion (cannot be removed for non-sharable resources), hold and wait (broken by requesting everything at once or releasing before requesting), no preemption (allow the OS to take resources back), circular wait (impose a total order on resource types and request in increasing order). Deadlock detection uses the wait-for graph (single instances: cycle search) or Banker-style algorithms (multiple instances). Recovery: terminate all or one at a time, or preempt with rollback; choose victims by priority, progress, number of resources held and the number of times already victimised to avoid starvation. A cycle in a resource-allocation graph is necessary for deadlock and sufficient only for single-instance resources."""),
           ("Resource-allocation graph reading",
            """Request edges point from a process to a resource, assignment edges from a resource to a process; with multiple instances a resource node has several dots. No cycle ⇒ no deadlock. A cycle with every resource single-instance ⇒ deadlock. A cycle with multi-instance resources ⇒ possible deadlock: check whether some process outside the cycle can finish and release an instance that breaks it. Claim edges (dashed) represent future requests in avoidance schemes."""),
       ])

deepen("os-memory-paging",
       proofs=[
           ("Page-table size and the number of levels",
            """For a virtual address of v bits, page size 2ᵖ bytes and page-table entries of e bytes: the offset needs p bits and the page number v − p bits, so there are 2^(v−p) entries, occupying 2^(v−p)·e bytes in a single-level table. For a multi-level table every level must fit in one page, so one level indexes log₂(2ᵖ/e) = p − log₂ e bits. The page number must be covered by the levels: the number of levels is ⌈(v − p)/(p − log₂ e)⌉. Example: v = 48, p = 13, e = 8: index bits per level 13 − 3 = 10, page number 35 bits, ⌈35/10⌉ = 4 levels. The memory used by the top-level table alone is 2^(bits of top index)·e."""),
           ("Effective memory access time with a TLB",
            """Let the TLB lookup take t, a memory access m and the hit ratio h. On a hit the translation costs t and the data access m: t + m. On a miss, a single-level page table adds one more memory access: t + m + m. Hence EAT = h(t + m) + (1 − h)(t + 2m) = t + m + (1 − h)m. With t = 20, m = 100, h = 0.9: 0.9·120 + 0.1·220 = 130 ns. For a k-level page table a miss costs t + (k + 1)m. If the lookups in the TLB and the page table are overlapped, the t term may disappear on the miss path — read the question for the intended model."""),
           ("First-fit, best-fit and worst-fit are not comparable in general; internal fragmentation of paging",
            """Counter-examples show that no placement strategy dominates: holes of 100, 500, 200, 300, 600 and requests 212, 417, 112, 426 are all satisfied by best-fit but not by first-fit or worst-fit, while other request sequences favour first-fit. Paging has no external fragmentation, but the last page of each process is on average half empty: internal fragmentation of about p/2 per process. Choosing the page size p trades the size of the page table (n/p entries of e bytes for a process of size n) against fragmentation (p/2): total overhead ne/p + p/2 is minimised at p = √(2ne), the classic result."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Address translation checklist",
            """Logical address = (page number, offset). Physical address = (frame number, offset). Number of frames = physical memory / page size; number of pages = virtual address space / page size. The page-table entry holds the frame number plus valid, dirty, reference and protection bits, which determines the minimal entry size. An **inverted page table** has one entry per frame and is searched by hashing the (process, page) pair. Segmentation maps (segment, offset) through a segment table with base and limit; segmented paging pages each segment. External fragmentation is removed by paging or by compaction (which requires dynamic relocation)."""),
           ("Fragmentation arithmetic",
            """Internal fragmentation of a process of size s with page size p is ⌈s/p⌉·p − s. The number of pages is ⌈s/p⌉. A buddy system allocates blocks in powers of two: a request of size s gets 2^⌈log₂ s⌉ bytes, so internal fragmentation is below 50%, while freed buddies merge into larger blocks. Slab allocation caches kernel objects of fixed sizes. Swapping a process out and in costs roughly twice its size divided by the transfer rate plus latency."""),
       ])

deepen("os-virtual-memory",
       proofs=[
           ("Effective access time with page faults",
            """Let p be the probability that a reference causes a page fault, m the memory access time and s the total fault-service time (disk access, page-table update, restart). Then EAT = (1 − p)m + p·s. With m = 200 ns and s = 8 ms = 8,000,000 ns: EAT = 200 + p(8,000,000 − 200). To keep the slowdown under 10% we need p·7,999,800 < 20, so p < 2.5 × 10⁻⁶, i.e. fewer than one fault in 400,000 accesses. Because the disk is about 10⁵–10⁶ times slower than memory, even a tiny fault rate dominates the EAT."""),
           ("LRU is a stack algorithm and has no Belady anomaly",
            """Let S(t, k) be the set of pages in memory after t references under LRU with k frames; LRU keeps the k most recently used distinct pages. Then S(t, k) ⊆ S(t, k + 1) for all t: the k most recently used pages are among the k + 1 most recently used. Because the content with k frames is always a subset of the content with k + 1 frames, any reference that hits with k frames also hits with k + 1: the number of faults is non-increasing in k. FIFO does not have the inclusion property: for the reference string 1 2 3 4 1 2 5 1 2 3 4 5 it produces 9 faults with 3 frames and 10 with 4 (Belady's anomaly). The optimal algorithm is also a stack algorithm."""),
           ("The optimal (Belady) replacement is optimal",
            """Suppose an algorithm A evicts a page other than the one used farthest in the future (call that page x) at the first point where it differs from OPT. Modify A to evict x instead. The pages kept differ only in that A′ keeps a page y that is used before x. Until the next reference to y both versions behave the same; at that reference A′ has y in memory (a hit) while A would fault — or if x is referenced first, A faults in A′ at the cost of one fault that A had avoided, but then A′ has already saved the fault at y: the number of faults of A′ is no larger than that of A. Repeating the exchange transforms any algorithm into OPT without increasing faults."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Thrashing and the working set",
            """Thrashing occurs when a process (or the system) spends more time paging than executing because its working set does not fit in memory. CPU utilisation first rises with the degree of multiprogramming and then collapses. The working-set model keeps in memory the pages referenced in the last Δ references; if the sum of working sets exceeds memory a process is suspended. Page-fault-frequency control adds or removes frames to keep the fault rate between two thresholds. Local replacement confines a process to its own frames; global replacement can steal from others but lets one process affect another."""),
           ("Algorithms and implementation notes",
            """Second-chance (clock) uses a reference bit and a circular pointer: if the bit is 0 evict, otherwise clear it and advance — an approximation of LRU. The enhanced version also uses the dirty bit (prefer clean pages). LFU/MFU use counters. Copy-on-write shares pages after fork until one process writes. A valid–invalid bit marks pages not in memory; the page-fault handler finds a frame, reads the page, updates the table and restarts the instruction. Page size trade-offs: larger pages shrink the page table and increase TLB reach but increase internal fragmentation and the time to read a page."""),
       ])

deepen("os-file-systems-disks",
       proofs=[
           ("Maximum file size with direct and indirect blocks",
            """Let the block size be B and a block pointer P bytes, so a block holds n = B/P pointers. An inode with d direct pointers addresses d blocks; a single-indirect pointer addresses one block of n pointers, hence n data blocks; a double-indirect pointer addresses n indirect blocks of n pointers each: n² data blocks; a triple-indirect pointer addresses n³. The maximum file has d + n + n² + n³ blocks, i.e. (d + n + n² + n³)·B bytes. For B = 1 KB, P = 4 bytes (n = 256), d = 10: 10 + 256 + 65,536 + 16,777,216 = 16,843,018 blocks ≈ 16.06 GB. The practical limit may be smaller if the file size field or pointer width cannot address that many blocks."""),
           ("Disk access time and the benefit of seek optimisation",
            """Access time = seek time + rotational latency + transfer time. On average the target sector is half a rotation away, so the average rotational latency is ½·(60/RPM) s: 4.17 ms at 7200 RPM. Transferring one sector takes (rotation time)/(sectors per track). With a 6 ms average seek, 200 sectors per track and 7200 RPM, one random sector costs 6 + 4.17 + 0.04 ≈ 10.2 ms. Because seek time dominates for random access, scheduling algorithms try to reduce the total head movement: for the queue 98, 183, 37, 122, 14, 124, 65, 67 with the head at 53, FCFS moves 640 cylinders while SSTF moves 236, SCAN (up first) 331 and LOOK (up first) 299."""),
           ("RAID capacity and fault tolerance",
            """With N identical disks of capacity C: RAID 0 stripes data and gives NC capacity with no redundancy (one failure loses everything); RAID 1 mirrors, giving NC/2 and surviving one failure per mirrored pair; RAID 5 stores one parity block per stripe, rotated across disks: capacity (N − 1)C. Parity P = D₁ ⊕ D₂ ⊕ … ⊕ D_{N−1}, so any single lost block equals the XOR of the others — recoverable. A small write to RAID 5 requires four I/Os (read old data and parity, write new data and parity) because P_new = P_old ⊕ D_old ⊕ D_new. RAID 6 uses two independent parities and tolerates two failures with capacity (N − 2)C."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("File allocation methods",
            """**Contiguous:** fast sequential and direct access, external fragmentation, files cannot easily grow. **Linked:** no external fragmentation, poor random access (n disk reads for block n), pointer overhead and fragile; **FAT** keeps the links in a table that can be cached. **Indexed:** an index block holds all block pointers, giving direct access; multi-level and combined schemes (Unix inode) handle large files. Directory implementations: linear list, hash table, tree. Free-space management: bit vector (n bits for n blocks), linked free list, grouping, counting. Journaling file systems write intended metadata updates to a log first so recovery is fast."""),
           ("Disk scheduling details",
            """SSTF can starve distant requests; SCAN (elevator) sweeps to the disk end then reverses, giving a uniform wait except at the ends; C-SCAN returns to the start without serving requests and gives more uniform waiting; LOOK and C-LOOK reverse at the last request instead of the disk end. Always read the initial direction and the disk size. Total head movement is the sum of absolute differences between consecutive positions, including any trip to the disk end for SCAN. SSDs have no seek or rotational latency but suffer from write amplification and wear."""),
       ])
